---
import TerminalLayout from '../../../layouts/TerminalLayout.astro';
import { getSeries } from '../../../lib/series';

const series = await getSeries('gas-city');
if (!series) {
  throw new Error('Series "gas-city" not found');
}
const firstPart = series.parts[0];
---

<TerminalLayout
  title={`${series.title} — mynameisjonas`}
  description={series.description}
  image={series.cover}
  type="article"
>
  <article>
    <p class="term-prompt"><a href="/blog">~/blog</a> $ cd gas-city/</p>

    <header class="post-header">
      <p class="post-series">[series &middot; {series.parts.length} parts]</p>
      <h1 class="post-title">{series.title}</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        <time datetime={firstPart.data.date.toISOString()}>{firstPart.data.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}</time>
        {firstPart.data.tags.map((tag) => <span>#{tag}</span>)}
      </p>
    </header>

    {series.cover && <img src={series.cover} alt={series.title} class="series-cover" />}

    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <div class="prose-intro">
        <p>This is a seven-part tutorial about building a working web app while learning Gas City. Each part adds something new on top of the last. By the end you have an RSS reader of your own and a mental model for how Gas City fits into a project.</p>
        <p>If you have used coding agents in your editor (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex), you already have most of the muscle this tutorial asks for. Gas City sits one level above that: instead of one agent helping you in your editor, you orchestrate several agents from the command line, each with its own role, and watch them deliver a feature together.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>What we are building</h2>
      <p>A minimalist Hacker-News-style RSS aggregator. It fetches the HN front-page feed, stores items in SQLite, renders them in a simple page, and ships small features along the way (per-source labels, search, a daily LLM-generated digest). The app is written by agents under your direction. The configs you write to direct them are the artifacts you keep.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A small Hono app on Bun, with <code>bun:sqlite</code>, <code>hono/html</code>, and HTMX.</li>
        <li>Five agents in your city: a mayor (always-on coordinator), a backend specialist, a DBA specialist, a frontend specialist, and a code reviewer.</li>
        <li>Three scheduled jobs: a periodic feed fetch, a nightly database prune, and a daily AI-written digest.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The stack is deliberately self-contained: no build step, no bundler, no JSX transpilation, no Tailwind toolchain. Bun runs <code>.ts</code> files directly, <code>bun:sqlite</code> is built in, <code>hono/html</code> is server-rendered, HTMX adds interactivity without leaving the server. Less to configure means less for the agents to get wrong.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="series-parts">
      {series.parts.map((post) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/blog/${post.id}`}>
            <span class="num">{String(post.data.part).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div>
              <span class="part-title">{post.data.title.replace(/^Part \d+:\s*/i, '')}</span>
              <p class="part-desc">{post.data.description}</p>
            </div>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </article>
</TerminalLayout>

<style>
  .series-cover {
    width: 100%; margin-bottom: 40px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .series-parts {
    list-style: none; margin: 48px 0 0; padding: 0 0 0 20px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .series-parts li + li { margin-top: 16px; }
  .series-parts a { display: flex; gap: 14px; }
  .num { width: 2ch; flex-shrink: 0; padding-top: 2px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .part-title { font-size: 17px; font-weight: 500; transition: color 150ms ease; }
  .series-parts a:hover .part-title { color: var(--accent); }
  .part-desc { margin-top: 4px; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--muted); }
</style>
